WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.949
Hey Thoughty2 here.

00:00:01.949 --> 00:00:04.060
Have you ever been cold?

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:08.330
And I don’t mean when your nipples get a
bit frosty, I’m talking about freezing,

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:11.070
frostbite-inducing cold.

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:17.480
The coldest settlement on Earth is Oymyakon
in Russia where temperatures average -50 degrees

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:20.310
C and can drop to -71 degrees.

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:26.650
At those temperatures if you cried, the tears
would instantly freeze your eyes shut, blinding

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:27.650
you.

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:31.849
And without proper clothing you would be dead
within the hour.

00:00:31.849 --> 00:00:38.210
But there’s a living organism that’s been
with us long before the dinosaurs, a creature

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:44.090
that has survived all five mass extinctions,
that would find these frigid temperatures

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:51.489
no problem at all; the 600-million-year-old
Tardigrade.

00:00:51.489 --> 00:00:55.899
Tardigrades are micro-animals, approximately
0.5mm in length.

00:00:55.899 --> 00:01:00.220
They have a digestive tract and eight little
legs but that’s where the similarities to

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:02.750
other animals ends.

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:06.340
Unlike the residents of Oymyakon who would
perish in minutes if they went outside without

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:16.520
at least 20 coats on, the Tardigrade can survive
at temperatures as low as -272 degrees Celsius.

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:24.140
That’s one degree above absolute zero, the
lowest temperature physically possible within

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:29.610
our universe.

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:35.120
Even when it can’t get any colder, the humble
Tardigrade just keeps on going.

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:39.470
The Tardigrade, sometimes called a “water
bear” because of its little claws, is the

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:42.900
most indestructible animal on the planet.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:46.140
They are practically immortal.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:50.710
As well as surviving the lowest temperatures
in the universe, tardigrades can survive heat

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:57.970
up to 150 degrees Celcius, pressure six times
greater than those found in the deepest ocean

00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:05.150
trenches on Earth and ironizing radiation
100 times higher than the lethal dose for

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:06.560
a human.

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:10.560
This little guy could literally chill out
inside the reactor core at Chernobyl and be

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:12.270
completely fine.

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:18.700
But most amazingly of all, and unique to this
creature, the tardigrade can go without any

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.140
food or water for over 30 years.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:23.750
But how?

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:27.470
Well, they die.

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:32.810
The tardigrade can dehydrate itself, like
a ready meal from back to the future, to the

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:36.510
point where it is less than 3% water.

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:39.590
The human body is 60% water.

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:46.490
In this ultra-deep hibernation, known as cryptobiosis,
the tardigrade has no need for any sustenance

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:52.460
and can sustain this state for 30 years, after
which it simply re-hydrates itself and comes

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:57.040
back to life, to forage for food and reproduce,
if it’s in the mood.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:02.130
They don’t technically die whilst dehydrated,
it’s more like a state of suspended animation.

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:07.100
A single tardigrade can live to be over a
century old.

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:12.540
NASA sent thousands of tardigrades into space
where they were sent out into the total vacuum

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:18.490
of space for days on end, where they were
subjected to massive solar radiation.

00:03:18.490 --> 00:03:20.650
They came back alive and well.

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:25.790
What’s more some of the tardigrades had
given birth to lots of little tardigrade babies,

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:32.640
out in the vacuum of space, the most inhospitable
place imaginable, and the space-babies were

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:37.040
also completely normal and healthy on their
return to Earth.

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:42.020
To date, they are the only animal we know
of that has gone defenseless into the total

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:45.650
vacuum of space and lived to tell the tale.

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:52.190
Some scientists even speculate that Tardigrades
are aliens and actually came to our planet

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:56.290
from another planet such as Mars, over a billion
years ago.

00:03:56.290 --> 00:04:01.360
Because their DNA is strange, and unlike anything
we’ve seen before.

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:07.880
But these aren’t some rare beast exclusively
found under a single rock in Antarctica.

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:14.400
Tardigrades are everywhere, odds are you are
only a few metres from a Tardigrade right

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:15.400
now.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:19.780
That’s because they live in mosses and lichens,
and where do you find moss?

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:22.350
Well it only covers the entire planet.

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:27.680
They feed on plant cells, so the moss and
lichen provides the perfect, cosy and damp

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:28.680
home.

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:34.080
Although they mostly eat plants, tardigrades
have been known to cannabalise each other.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:39.720
And they’re not exclusive to moss either,
they have also been found at the top of the

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:44.900
tallest mountains in the Himalayas, inside
hot springs in Japan, at the very bottom of

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:48.810
the ocean and deep within the Antarctic wilderness.

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:52.250
So basically, everywhere.

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:54.680
Tardigrades just don’t care about anything.

00:04:54.680 --> 00:05:00.780
One of the most amazing tardigrade abilities
is how they deal with the extreme cold.

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:06.820
At extremely low temperatures ice crystals
can form inside the cells of a life form,

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:11.830
ripping apart the cell’s vital components
such as the DNA, which is always fatal to

00:05:11.830 --> 00:05:13.490
that organism.

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:16.250
Some animals that have evolved to cope with
the extreme cold.

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:22.960
For example, fish in cold regions make antifreeze
that lowers the freezing point of their cells,

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:28.370
stopping ice from forming and preventing fatal
frost damage.

00:05:28.370 --> 00:05:34.300
Scientists have found no evidence of antifreeze
inside tardigrades, they’re way too badass

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:36.060
for that.

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:42.419
Instead tardigrades simply tolerate ice forming
inside their cells, they just don’t care.

00:05:42.419 --> 00:05:48.070
Amazingly, they are able to quickly repair
any damage caused by the ice crystals by rapidly

00:05:48.070 --> 00:05:50.290
creating antioxidants.

00:05:50.290 --> 00:05:55.570
They also produce nucleating agents that encourage
ice to form around the cell instead of inside

00:05:55.570 --> 00:05:56.570
it.

00:05:56.570 --> 00:06:02.860
But what is truly baffling about this tiny
immortal bear, is how it deals with extreme

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:04.030
heat.

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:10.440
At temperatures as high as 150 degrees Celcius,
a cell’s membrane should unravel and it

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:16.250
would be torn apart, this would happen to
all cells simultaneously, quickly killing

00:06:16.250 --> 00:06:19.020
the cells’ host.

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:27.010
Tardigrades have been heated to 150 degrees
C for 15 minutes and what happened?

00:06:27.010 --> 00:06:28.810
Absolutely nothing.

00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:34.630
It just carried on scuttling around as though
it had been quietly sunbathing.

00:06:34.630 --> 00:06:40.889
Scientists still have no idea how they do
this, it shouldn’t be possible for cells

00:06:40.889 --> 00:06:44.930
to survive at these temperatures, yet they
do.

00:06:44.930 --> 00:06:49.510
Naturally, scientists are now studying the
microscopic tardigrade to try and unlock the

00:06:49.510 --> 00:06:52.000
secrets of its immortality.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:54.630
Maybe we can steal a secret or two for ourselves.

00:06:54.630 --> 00:06:55.629
Thanks for watching.

